{
  "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
  "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: If it had been the other way around and the district court had said what appellate court said and the appellate court had said what the district court said, what would you be arguing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I can understand if it's a question of judgment, a matter of judgment about this case the court of appeals is supposed to say: District judge, it's your view that matters here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Excuse me. You're saying they can't disagree with the policy. They can only -- only say there are special facts in this case that were not taken into account in the policy. But you're saying they are bound by the policy set forth in the Guidelines. That's not my understanding of either Apprendi or Booker."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DREEBEN: Reasonableness requires more of a balance of the policies and a consideration of the overall goal of the system of achieving uniformity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Under 3553(a), district courts have to provide reasons anyway, right."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Mr. Dreeben, can I ask another question? I go back to percentages for just -- to illustrate the point."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DREEBEN: Well, my response -- and if I could answer Mr. Chief Justice -- is that the fundamental point of this Court's Apprendi line of cases is that, so long as the statutory maximum is legally available to the judge, the judge can find facts within that range that justify the sentence, and that's all the Booker remedial opinion authorizes judges to do."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Green",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Stevens, the Eighth Circuit, even before the Guidelines, even before the Booker case, in 1993, in 1999, in cases called One Star and Decora respectively, went down from even higher levels to probation based upon the particular facts of the case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Green",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9311cc2b14ff-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We would ask the Court to overturn the judgment of the Eighth Circuit and abandon the extraordinary circumstances test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-7949",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "9311cc2b14ff11b8f5ffa99139d3016ed36a3f3f43b73130cdd2ba99d5ab23be",
